      "text": "Three proposed primary stimuli for muscle protein synthesis and hypertrophy:"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-STRENGTH-POWER.md#033",
      "path": [
        "03-STRENGTH-POWER.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:sports-science:03-strength-power, git-history:sports-science:03-strength-power]",
        "canonical_path": "sports-science/03-STRENGTH-POWER.md",
        "concepts": "[strength, power]",
        "current_path": "sports-science/03-STRENGTH-POWER.md",
        "id": "maxim:sports-science:strength-power",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "sports-science",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[strength, power]",
        "section": "sports-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Strength and Power: Neuromuscular Adaptations",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n1. MECHANICAL TENSION:\n   The primary driver. High mechanical load → integrin signaling\n   → mTORC1 activation → protein synthesis upregulation.\n   This is why heavy resistance training is the most reliable hypertrophy stimulus.\n   The muscle cell is literally being stretched under load;\n   mechanosensors (integrins, titin) detect this and signal."
    },
    {
      "id": "03-STRENGTH-POWER.md#034",
      "path": [
        "03-STRENGTH-POWER.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:sports-science:03-strength-power, git-history:sports-science:03-strength-power]",
        "canonical_path": "sports-science/03-STRENGTH-POWER.md",
        "concepts": "[strength, power]",
        "current_path": "sports-science/03-STRENGTH-POWER.md",
        "id": "maxim:sports-science:strength-power",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "sports-science",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[strength, power]",
        "section": "sports-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Strength and Power: Neuromuscular Adaptations",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "2. METABOLIC STRESS:\n   Metabolite accumulation (lactate, H⁺, Pi, adenosine) during\n   high-rep, moderate-load training.\n   Mechanism: uncertain but possibly involves:\n     Hypoxia within the muscle (occlusion of blood flow at high muscle pressure).\n     Reactive oxygen species (ROS) as signaling molecules.\n     Hormonal response (growth hormone, IGF-1).\n   This explains why blood-flow restriction (BFR) training at low loads\n   produces hypertrophy comparable to heavy lifting."
    },
    {
      "id": "03-STRENGTH-POWER.md#035",
      "path": [
        "03-STRENGTH-POWER.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:sports-science:03-strength-power, git-history:sports-science:03-strength-power]",
        "canonical_path": "sports-science/03-STRENGTH-POWER.md",
        "concepts": "[strength, power]",
        "current_path": "sports-science/03-STRENGTH-POWER.md",
        "id": "maxim:sports-science:strength-power",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "sports-science",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[strength, power]",
        "section": "sports-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Strength and Power: Neuromuscular Adaptations",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "3. MUSCLE DAMAGE:\n   Eccentric exercise causes microtrauma to muscle fibers.\n   Inflammatory response → satellite cell activation → repair and growth.\n   Role may be smaller than previously thought (some evidence suggests\n   muscle damage is a side effect, not necessary stimulus).\n   CURRENT CONSENSUS: Mechanical tension is dominant.\n                       Metabolic stress contributes.\n                       Muscle damage may contribute marginally.\n```"
    },
